# Answer: share of Experimental Economics studies conducted in a lab setting

**Headline (data-grounded hypothesis).** Within the Experimental Economics (ee) subset of the
meta-regression study data, **18 of 18 studies (100%, 18/18; Wilson 95% CI 82.4%–100%;
Clopper–Pearson exact 95% CI 81.5%–100%) were conducted in a lab (in-person) setting**, using the
original-study criterion `online.o == 0` at the unique `id` (O/R pair = replicated original study)
level. All 18 rows also have `online.r == 0`, so the replication side, the both-sides definition
(S1) and the triple-evidence definition (S2: `online.o==0` & `subjects.o ∈ {students,community}`
& `compensation.o ∈ {cash,credit}`) give the identical 18/18 — the estimate is robust to the
definition, but only because `online.o` and `online.r` are *constant at 0* in ee (zero within-ee
variance), so no alternative field-based definition could move it.

**Why the number is a boundary value.** `online.o` takes the single value 0 in all 18 ee rows and
`online.r` likewise 0 in all 18 (missing: 0/18). Any proportion computed from this field must
therefore be exactly 0 or 1; here it is 1. It is reported unsmoothed — with n = 18 the CI lower
bound (81.5–82.4%) is the honest statement of precision, and one-sided exact binomial p vs p=0.5
is 3.8e-06.

**Uncertainty / limitations.**
1. The data dictionary has **no "lab" boolean**; `online == 0` is a *proxy* for "in-person lab
   data collection", not a documented definition. Non-online could still mean a classroom
   paper-and-pencil session, a field/framing experiment, or administrative-data tasks; conversely
   `online == 1` can describe lab terminals used for recruitment.
2. `lab_id` is 100% missing in ee (0/18), so there is no internal way to validate the proxy.
3. `us_lab.o` / `us_lab.r` were **verified not to encode lab status**: they equal
   `experiment_country == "United States"` on all 927 rows (agreement = 1.0000), including 40 rows
   (original side) and 115 rows (replication side) that are coded `online == 1`. They are location
   flags and were excluded as lab evidence.
4. n = 18 (one row per O/R pair); no exclusions were needed — the exclusion log
   (`missing_or_contradictory.csv`) is explicitly empty ("NONE": no missing `online.o`, and no
   corroborating field contradicts `online.o == 0`). The only deviation from the modal
   "students" pattern is ee.4 (Charness & Dufwenberg 2011, `subjects.o == community`), which is
   still non-online and cash-compensated, hence lab-classified under every definition.
5. Denominators are unaffected by `drop` / `aggregated`: ee has 0 rows with `drop == True` and no
   aggregated rows.

**Cross-project positioning (file-1 labels only, unique-id dedup, drop-ids excluded | included).**
Under the same original-side criterion: ee 18/18 = 100%; rpp 89/90 = 98.9% | 93/96 = 96.9%;
ml1 12/13 = 92.3% | 15/16 = 93.8%; ml3 10/10 = 100%. ee is thus at the ceiling of a pattern in
which original studies across all four replication projects are overwhelmingly in-person; the
projects differ mainly on the *replication* side, where ml1/ml3 ran a sizeable share of data
collection online (lab-row level: ml1 432/576 = 75.0% in-person, ml3 200/210 = 95.2%), whereas
both ee sides are entirely in-person. ml1/ml3 are not structurally comparable to ee: each id there
spans ~37 (ml1) / ~21 (ml3) laboratory rows with mixed online/offline modes, and their
`aggregated == True` rows are coded `online.r == 0` in 26/26 cases despite at least one online lab
of the same id, so aggregated replication-side values must not be read as "fully in-person".
File 2 contributes no situational fields; it was used only to confirm coverage — 18/18 ee studies
match its "Experimental Economics" rows by first-author + year (12/18 pairs differ on sample-size
convention, which is why sample size was never used as a join key).

**Hypothesis sentence.** *In the Experimental Economics replication project essentially all
studies — 18/18, i.e. 100% (95% CI 82–100%) — were conducted in a lab setting: every ee row is
coded non-online with student/community subjects paid cash, so within this dataset the ee evidence
base is a purely in-person, university-lab one, and its low replication rate cannot be attributed
to online-vs-lab differences in data collection; the caveat is that "lab" is proxied by
`online == 0` because the dataset contains no direct lab indicator.*
